  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn inland-penetrating Atmospheric River (AR) coupled with a deep\nupper trough spreading into the Great Basin later tonight through\nSunday will bring widespread rain and high elevation snow to the\nforecast area over the next 36 hours. This AR is currently making\nlandfall with widespread precipitation quickly spreading across \nthe lower deserts of southern CA and AZ. This moisture will \nquickly spread across the forecast area from south to north \novernight, and persist through Sunday morning. \n\nThe forecast has remained fairly consistent over the past couple \nof days, with locally upwards of 3\" of additional rain across \norographically favored areas of southern Utah from the Pine \nValleys east to Boulder Mountain, and 1+ inches of rain in lower\nelevations including St George, Cedar City, Zion Canyon and Kanab.\nGiven recent rainfall over the past few weeks, antecedent \nconditions are nearly saturated and runoff is expected to be very \nefficient, resulting in another round of flooding across southern \nand east central Utah beginning late tonight, and continuing \nthrough Sunday. \n\nAs the region of enhanced IVT shifts east of the forecast area \nSunday afternoon, the upstream trough will spread into the Great\nBasin, with increasing large scale ascent interacting with an\nalready saturated airmass to maintain widespread precipitation\nthrough the afternoon and into the evening. Cooling temperatures\naloft will begin lowering snow levels from west to east during the\nafternoon, with snow levels falling below 8000 feet by late Sunday\nevening across nearly the entire forecast area excluding the far\nsoutheast. As this trough progresses into the central Rockies, a\nfavorable period of post-frontal orographics looks to persist\nalong the I-15 corridor through Sunday night, before gradually\nwinding down Monday morning. Total snow accumulation looks to run\nin the 3-6\" range below 9000 feet, while higher elevations will be\npushing 12\"+, and perhaps upwards of 20\" in the upper Cottonwoods.\nLow elevation rainfall totals will likely exceed 1\" across most\nnorthern valleys including much of the Wasatch Front through\nMonday morning.\n\nMid level ridging will bring temporary dry and stable conditions\nlate Monday through Tuesday, with a more progressive wave forecast\nto translate through the region Wednesday into Thursday. This\nquick-hitting wave will lack the moisture tap of recent events,\nthus precipitation totals will remain fairly light. Long range\nmodels lean heavily toward longwave ridging building into the\nregion next weekend into early in the following week.",
